#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106603} :activity/id #uuid "c5f2642e-6a87-5aeb-a038-d51e520f5f7e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050490 :topic/id #uuid "4d8d1b92-7074-5d18-8e35-15cd34d42567" :topic/math-academy-id 1377} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107069 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ below.\n\n![](images/00/004265d2269924c6ce8905b6b92e9050b84066b3b5a3cc5383c75dd08e62f413.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to3}\\log_2{x} = \\log_2{3}\\approx 1.58.\n$$\n\nFor logarithmic functions, the limit at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, so for any base $b \\gt 1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\log_b{x} = \\log_b{c}.\n$$\n\nRemember that logarithms are only defined for positive inputs $x\\gt 0,$ so we must have $c>0.$ Otherwise, the limit does not exist $\\textrm{(DNE}).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e274ae6b-34e4-5fe4-be2f-c7a278dfa917" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2891 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "163cb50e-cc52-5928-a53e-74d0de7c5711" :step/math-academy-id 15581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105580 :question/id #uuid "b18540cc-5822-5620-a36f-60cfbbf3196b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4255"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564525}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "2209645b-5517-5350-9f59-734ba0a67972" :step/math-academy-id 15582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107070 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look again at the function $y=\\log_2(x).$\n\n![](images/30/30a66b56ea1422fc61941d22aedbbf3f17e06810e66e6590490bd4b7188fd361.png)\n\nAs $x$ gets larger and larger, the curve increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\log_2{x}= \\infty.\n$$\n\nThe curve is not defined for $x\\leq 0,$ but as $x$ approaches zero from the right the curve *decreases* without bound. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\log_2{x} = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE},\\qquad\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to0}\\log_2{x} = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nThese properties are true for logarithms of any base $b\\gt 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "12a53922-65d6-5add-afe1-cbf5afcf1f1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2892 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits and Limits at Infinity of Logarithmic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "ddeebcf7-8414-5bf9-aa2c-92ea6d072894" :step/math-academy-id 15584 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105581 :question/id #uuid "16cc98d7-5998-5727-a5c8-659e88dc158e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f956a45f-29cb-5b78-a29f-3796e22af09d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564521}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Limits at Infinity of Logarithmic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2bb03a3f-9645-5c0b-9b4b-51be0f4e67c5" :step/math-academy-id 15585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106607 :ste